            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "I think this _might_ be a tsc bug, or at least something that other LSPs do that tsc could implement/improve.\n\nOnce it completes processing a request, it should be able to look forward through the queue and remove requests that have cancel requests following it in the pipeline.\n\nThis isn't backpressure specifically but it is how LSP servers can do less work (waste less work) while still performing all the still-in-flight requested work.\n\nIf you want to help alleviate this symptom, you can increase the text debounce for tsc:\n\n```lua\nvim.lsp.config('tsc', {\n  { flags = { debounce_text_changes = 500 },\n}\n```\n\nIt's in milliseconds, adjust according to what feels right. I haven't looked at helix but it's likely they have a larger debounce default for sending text changes/requesting updates.\n\nMy thoughts on ways to potentially improve this:\n\nOne (possible) bug I've seen is that if there's a text change but the last one was more than a debounce interval in the past, it'll immediately trigger the change and update requests. When someone starts typing, this means the first character automatically starts a chain of didChange/update requests and then the following changes are debounced, but that first one will always hit the server queue. The follow-ups always send cancel requests for the ones still pending at the server (but tsc ignores those--see above) so normally shouldn't tax the server any more than larger debounces would, but /shrug.\n\nWe could also look into a second level debounce specifically for the feature/capability update requests (semantic tokens, diagnostics, etc) that only fire when text changes appear to settle, Server-initiated pushes could occur more often than our pull requests in this case.  @mfussenegger for color on this.\n\nIf we did a second level debounce, we could also do something like backpressure for servers that don't respect cancel requests and build them up. didChange notifications are notifications, so we have no backpressure signal, but the feature requests are. If we aren't getting replies, we can increase that second level debounce exponentially to a much higher max.",
